{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA recurring defect shape — a check whose PASS/absent/empty result is indistinguishable from\nthe check never having run — appeared nine times in one session (2026-08-20/21). Code review\nhas no detection language for it. Per the refinement comment the mirror is included: a broken\ninstrument can also produce a confident FAILING result for a reason other than the condition,\nso the language covers \"any definite result\", not just PASS.\n\n**OPERATOR RULING (ticket comment, 2026-08-21): do NOT create a new criterion.** Add the\nfail-open / producer-liveness language to the EXISTING `tests` criterion (test-oracle\npopulation) and `error-handling` criterion (shipped-code population); a new criterion only if\ncalibration proves the language cannot fire from within those hosts.\n\n### The nine instances (calibration corpus; full narratives in this ticket's comments)\n\n`tests` host: (1) `sound-bumpkinly-thrush` — absence asserted on a child's output; a\nsignal-killed child writes nothing, so never-ran reports GREEN; (2) `abusive-pebbly-sakimonkey`\n— a convergence test re-derived the send path's dedup key instead of driving it; (3) a harness\ngrepped `\"^FAILED\"` against colorized pytest output — \"0 failed\" regardless; (4) an orphaned\nsweep's truncated log and a clean log produce the same empty grep.\n\n`error-handling` host: (5) `obvious-saline-gerbil` — health check never read the returncode;\n(6) `defiable-distortive-pinniped` — `gen_env_registry` failed open on a helper missing from\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`; (7) `blocky-matricidal-squid` — the `os.environ` half, same posture;\n(8) a `pgrep -f \"make test\"` monitor matched its own shell; (9) a `pgrep -f \"review-plan\"`\nwaiter matched its own process.\n\n## Approach\n\nEach host criterion's rubric already has a home:\n\n- `tests`: the full rubric lives in `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`. Add a\n  **fail-open oracle** antipattern: an ABSENCE assertion (`not in`, `assert not`, `== \"\"`,\n  empty-collection) derived from a subprocess/file/network/captured output, or a grep/regex\n  over another tool's output used as a pass/fail signal, with NO liveness check on the producer\n  (exit status, non-empty capture, an asserted presence). Litmus (the ticket's reviewer prompt,\n  broadened per the refinement comment): *name every world in which the check produces its\n  definite result — pass or fail; if \"the producer never ran / emitted nothing\" is among them,\n  the check cannot distinguish its verdict from its own failure.* Qualify FP guard (iii)\n  (\"greps of COMMAND OUTPUT are valid\") to require established producer liveness, and add the\n  matching FP guard for absence assertions preceded by a liveness assertion.\n- `error-handling` is a BASE-REVIEWER dimension (per its criteria_routing.json `_comment`), so\n  its language goes in `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` §\"Surface grounded\n  findings\": a short fail-open paragraph naming the shipped-code triggers — a silent\n  lookup/membership MISS path, a subprocess check that never reads the returncode, a\n  `pgrep`/`pkill` pattern that can match the matching process itself, any best-effort check\n  whose failure is invisible — with the same litmus and the uniform remediation (assert the\n  producer RAN before trusting any absence derived from it). The paragraph instructs the base\n  reviewer to TAG such findings `error-handling` (routing hosts them @0.50), and delimits\n  against the security overlay's same-named \"fail-open error handling\" antipattern\n  (code-review-security.md): security-consequence fail-open (authz/validation fallback) stays\n  with security; this language covers checks/oracles/health probes whose failure is invisible.\n- Registry record: extend the `_comment` of the `tests` and `error-handling` entries in\n  `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` (this ticket + trigger list + placement\n  ruling). No posture/threshold/blocking values change.\n- Calibration corpus: add the nine instances as seeded-diff recall cases (pre-fix,\n  `expect: finding`) each paired with a post-fix no-fire case (`expect: pass`) — instances 1–4\n  in `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml`, 5–9 in\n  `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml`, dso-corpus house style.\n- Docs: code-review criteria have no per-criterion `rebar explain <id>` section (`_explain` in\n  `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py` covers plan-review criteria + guides only); the code-review\n  author surface is `rebar explain review` (`src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md`).\n  Default taken (see fork-record comment): document the pattern there with two worked examples\n  (instances 1 and 6).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` — fail-open oracle antipattern + FP guard.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` — error-handling fail-open paragraph.\n- `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` — `_comment` provenance only.\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` — 4 recall + 4 no-fire cases.\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml` — 5 recall + 5 no-fire cases.\n- `src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md` — pattern + two worked examples.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Offline (no model): `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py` — both extended specs must\n  stay strict-clean (`validate_eval_spec(strict=True)`, registered scorers, every case has a\n  `diff`).\n- Live calibration replay (bedrock via `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`): run each of the 18 new cases\n  through its Pass-1 prompt via `rebar.llm.evals.eval_solver.run_case` (`code-review-tests` for\n  1–4, `code-review-base` for 5–9). Each seeded pre-fix diff is MINIMAL — the fail-open defect\n  is the only defect in it — so a grounded finding on it is attributable by construction.\n  Decision rule: a pre-fix case passes iff it yields ≥1 finding AND the finding's quoted claim\n  names the seeded fail-open defect (the quote is copied into the calibration comment, so the\n  attribution is auditable); a pre-fix case whose only findings are about something else counts\n  as a MISS. A post-fix case passes iff it yields ZERO findings (the `code_review_no_fire`\n  presence/absence rule). If a post-fix case fires an UNRELATED finding, the seeded diff\n  carries an incidental defect — revise the diff to remove it (keeping the liveness fix\n  intact) and re-run until zero-finding; if the finding IS about producer liveness, the\n  seeded fix is wrong — correct it and re-run. Every revision + final per-case result is\n  recorded in the calibration comment.\n- FP posture: both hosts are ALREADY blocking (`tests` @0.54, `error-handling` @0.50) so no\n  advisory ramp applies; the FP measurement is the 9 post-fix no-fire cases plus the specs'\n  standing no-fire families staying silent in the same replay. Record both.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `code-review-tests.md` names the fail-open oracle antipattern with the producer-liveness\n      litmus and matching FP guard; checked by `rg fail-open` on the file.\n- [ ] `code-review-base.md` carries the error-handling fail-open language (silent lookup miss,\n      unchecked returncode, pgrep self-match, invisible best-effort failure); checked by\n      `rg fail-open` on the file.\n- [ ] The `tests` and `error-handling` `_comment` fields in\n      `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` record the triggers + this ticket; no\n      routing VALUE changes (checked by diff).\n- [ ] Calibration: all 9 pre-fix cases yield ≥1 finding whose quoted claim names the seeded\n      fail-open defect, and all 9 post-fix cases yield ZERO findings (with the unrelated-fire\n      fallback protocol above); per-case quotes + results recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] FP posture recorded in the same comment (hosts already blocking; standing no-fire\n      families silent).\n- [ ] `rebar explain review` documents the pattern with ≥2 worked examples; checked by\n      `rebar explain review | rg -c \"fail-open\"` ≥ 1 plus reading the examples.\n- [ ] Eval specs strict-clean offline; checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787354542724140001,"uuid":"3d88c5c3-f765-4642-9270-4876cd5dbaeb"}